<?xml version="1.0"?>
<!--
  ClickHouse system-log retention.  ROLE 1 (infra) owns this file.

  *** NOT CURRENTLY MOUNTED — see "HOW TO APPLY IT SAFELY" below. ***

  WHY THIS EXISTS
    ClickHouse ships its own diagnostic log tables with NO TTL by default. Only
    processors_profile_log had one on this deployment. Left alone they grow without
    bound: measured 2026-09-29, the `system` database held 35.72 GiB against 14.72 MiB
    of actual Langfuse data in `default` — a ratio of roughly 2,400:1. trace_log alone
    was 19.65 GiB / 931 M rows, taking ~12 M profiler samples per day.

  WHY IT IS A CONFIG FILE AND NOT `ALTER TABLE`
    The TTLs were first applied with ALTER TABLE ... MODIFY TTL, which works and
    survives a restart — but ClickHouse (re)creates system log tables from THIS config
    at startup, so a version upgrade that recreates them would silently drop an
    ALTER-applied TTL and the growth would resume unnoticed. Declaring them here makes
    the retention a property of the deployment rather than of the current table.

  COLUMN NAMES ARE NOT UNIFORM
    Most system log tables partition on `event_date`. opentelemetry_span_log does NOT —
    it uses `finish_date`, and an event_date TTL on it fails with
    `Code: 47 ... Missing columns: 'event_date'`. Verified per-table before writing.

  RETENTION RATIONALE
     3d  trace_log            profiler samples; only useful while debugging live
     7d  text_log             server log lines; a week covers a weekend incident
     7d  opentelemetry_span_log
    14d  metric_log, asynchronous_metric_log, part_log   trend/merge debugging
    30d  query_log            small (~150 MiB) and genuinely useful for slow queries
    30d  processors_profile_log  pre-existing default, left as-is
     7d  everything else      small today, unbounded tomorrow

  HOW TO APPLY IT SAFELY  (this bit was learned the hard way, 2026-09-29)
    Mounting this file requires recreating the clickhouse service, and that is only safe
    when the merge backlog has drained. It was first applied while ~870 inactive parts
    (~32 GiB) were still pending cleanup from the initial ALTER-applied TTLs. On restart
    ClickHouse re-scanned that backlog and resumed materialising the 14-day TTL across
    asynchronous_metric_log's 4.6 BILLION rows, which pegged the OrbStack VM at ~500% CPU
    for over half an hour, took the Docker daemon unresponsive, and took down every other
    containerised workload on the machine — not just Langfuse. The mount was reverted.

    Before mounting, confirm the backlog is drained:
      SELECT active, count(), formatReadableSize(sum(bytes_on_disk))
      FROM system.parts WHERE database='system' GROUP BY active;
    Inactive should be small (well under 1 GiB). Then:
      cd ~/Documents/1-projects/langfuse
      # re-add the bind mount to docker-compose.override.yml (see git history of this repo)
      docker compose up -d clickhouse

    Note the reclaim does NOT depend on this file. The ALTER TABLE ... MODIFY TTL
    statements already applied took `system` from 35.72 GiB to 3.04 GiB and persist across
    restarts. This file only protects against a future VERSION UPGRADE recreating the
    system tables from config and silently dropping those TTLs.

  TO CHANGE RETENTION
    Edit here, then recreate the clickhouse service with BOTH compose files:
      cd ~/Documents/1-projects/langfuse
      docker compose up -d clickhouse
    Confirm with:
      SELECT name, extract(engine_full,'TTL[^S]*') FROM system.tables
      WHERE database='system' AND engine LIKE '%MergeTree%';
-->
<clickhouse>
    <trace_log>
        <ttl>event_date + INTERVAL 3 DAY DELETE</ttl>
    </trace_log>

    <text_log>
        <ttl>event_date + INTERVAL 7 DAY DELETE</ttl>
    </text_log>

    <!-- finish_date, NOT event_date -->
    <opentelemetry_span_log>
        <ttl>finish_date + INTERVAL 7 DAY DELETE</ttl>
    </opentelemetry_span_log>

    <metric_log>
        <ttl>event_date + INTERVAL 14 DAY DELETE</ttl>
    </metric_log>

    <asynchronous_metric_log>
        <ttl>event_date + INTERVAL 14 DAY DELETE</ttl>
    </asynchronous_metric_log>

    <part_log>
        <ttl>event_date + INTERVAL 14 DAY DELETE</ttl>
    </part_log>

    <query_log>
        <ttl>event_date + INTERVAL 30 DAY DELETE</ttl>
    </query_log>

    <processors_profile_log>
        <ttl>event_date + INTERVAL 30 DAY DELETE</ttl>
    </processors_profile_log>

    <!-- Small today, unbounded tomorrow. Capped so this does not recur. -->
    <query_views_log>
        <ttl>event_date + INTERVAL 7 DAY DELETE</ttl>
    </query_views_log>

    <query_metric_log>
        <ttl>event_date + INTERVAL 7 DAY DELETE</ttl>
    </query_metric_log>

    <asynchronous_insert_log>
        <ttl>event_date + INTERVAL 7 DAY DELETE</ttl>
    </asynchronous_insert_log>

    <backup_log>
        <ttl>event_date + INTERVAL 30 DAY DELETE</ttl>
    </backup_log>

    <error_log>
        <ttl>event_date + INTERVAL 30 DAY DELETE</ttl>
    </error_log>

    <background_schedule_pool_log>
        <ttl>event_date + INTERVAL 7 DAY DELETE</ttl>
    </background_schedule_pool_log>
</clickhouse>
